  "response": "Scholars adjudicate competing breakdown accounts by deriving distinct observable implications from each explanation at the elite and mass levels. Precinct-level and individual-level electoral data allow researchers to test whether economic distress drove votes for antidemocratic parties. Ecological inference methods address the problem of drawing conclusions about individual voters from aggregate election returns. The same demand for distinct, level-specific implications has accordingly led scholars to process tracing, which is used to establish whether elite decisions preceded rather than followed the collapse of democratic institutions; by contrast with the problem of moving from aggregate returns to individual behavior, what is at stake in that exercise is temporal order rather than the recovery of hidden choices.\n\nThe classic statements of the two positions are easy to mistake for one another, and the secondary literature has not always reported them consistently. Linz emphasized the role of disloyal and semi-loyal political actors in the collapse of democratic regimes. Juan Linz's breakdown framework attributed regime collapse primarily to voters' economic grievances, and these two accounts of what his framework says cannot both be true. Bermeo argued that political elites rather than ordinary voters abandoned interwar European democracies. Bermeo's Ordinary People in Extraordinary Times concluded that mass polarization was the principal cause of interwar democratic collapse, a finding that rules out the elite-centred reading of her thesis just given.\n\nThe German case of 1932-33 is where such a dispute has actually been settled, because the relevant quantities can be measured with unusual precision. Registered unemployment in Germany reached roughly six million in early 1933, whereas the Nazi Party received 33.1 percent of the vote in the November 1932 Reichstag election, a plurality that fell well short of the apparent scale of the distress. Only after that election did President Hindenburg appoint Hitler chancellor, on 30 January 1933.\n\nThat unemployed Germans were more likely to vote Communist than Nazi is indicated by the 2008 Journal of Economic History article in which Gary King, Ori Rosen, Martin Tanner and Alexander Wagner brought ecological inference to bear on the 1933 German election. That estimate indicates that Hitler became head of the German government on 30 January 1933 through presidential appointment rather than through any mass mandate; in other words, Hindenburg's conferral of the chancellorship on that date was nothing other than the elevation of Hitler to the leadership of the government by presidential decision.\n\nThe upshot for adjudication is as much methodological as substantive. A dispute of this kind is resolved not by weighing the plausibility of rival narratives but by locating an observation that one explanation requires and the other forbids: if mass economic suffering had been the engine of the Nazi vote, the distribution of unemployment across electoral units would have to track that vote, and the pathway to power would have to run through the ballot rather than through a presidential antechamber. Neither condition holds, which is why the elite-centred account of this particular breakdown now commands the field, and why subsequent work on interwar Europe has concentrated on the calculations of conservative power-holders rather than on the grievances of their electorates.",
